A UDP recvfrom that fails EPERM tears the socket down, as ENOTCONN already
does. The macOS App Sandbox refuses a reply to an unconnected UDP socket
in an app with network.client but not network.server: the datagram stays
queued, poll keeps reporting the socket readable, and slirp, which kept the
socket and answered the guest with an ICMP error, read it again forever,
spinning QEMU's main loop with the BQL held. Detaching costs that one reply;
the guest's next packet makes a fresh socket. Also frees the mbuf the
ENOTCONN branch leaked.

diff --git a/src/socket.c b/src/socket.c
index 8ca61d7..6335ffb 100644
--- a/src/socket.c
+++ b/src/socket.c
@@ -664,12 +664,18 @@ void sorecvfrom(struct socket *so)
         DEBUG_MISC(" did recvfrom %d, errno = %d-%s", m->m_len, errno,
                    g_strerror(errno));
         if (m->m_len < 0) {    	
-            if (errno == ENOTCONN) {
+            if (errno == ENOTCONN || errno == EPERM) {
                 /*
                  * UDP socket got burnt, e.g. by suspend on iOS. Tear it down
                  * and let it get re-created if the guest still needs it
+                 *
+                 * EPERM: the macOS sandbox refused the datagram (an app with
+                 * network.client but not network.server, on a socket that
+                 * never connect()ed). It stays queued and poll keeps saying
+                 * readable, so a socket kept open spins the main loop.
                  */
                 udp_detach(so);
+                m_free(m);
             } else {
                 /* Report error as ICMP */
                 switch (so->so_lfamily) {
